  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:WIEWvlu91jg",
    "purpose": "A history of Eddie Bauer as a down outerwear maker, told through vintage jackets the creator wears and examines, from the brand's tackle-shop origins to its sale in 1968.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Under Eddie Bauer himself, the brand was arguably America's greatest outdoor clothing maker. Its down jackets, including the Skyliner, the B-9 flight parka, the Karakoram (K2) jacket and the Everest Parka, set the standard. Crescent Down Works is presented as the modern spiritual successor.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Eddie Bauer started with a tackle shop that restrung rackets and made feather shuttlecocks, which got him into sourcing feathers.",
      "He made his first down jacket after nearly dying of hypothermia. He had handed off his wool coat because it was too heavy, so the goal was to save weight, not add warmth.",
      "In WWII he coordinated cold-weather gear from other American outdoor brands, collected feathers through Ducks Unlimited, and became a major military supplier of sleeping bags and pack boards.",
      "The B-9 parka was built to keep pilots warm at -70° for 3 hours. It uses a down and feather mix and is heavy and hard to compress. It has double underarm gussets, a short zipper for sitting, a mouton collar that converts to a hood, and a neck wind blocker.",
      "The Karakoram (K2) jacket came from the 1953 K2 expedition, including the fall arrested by Pete Schoening. The Everest Parka used nylon and velcro and has a wolverine and coyote fur hood.",
      "Eddie Bauer sold the company to his partner in 1968. A 1970s avocado Karakoram Expedition jacket shows the move to a fur-free down hood.",
      "Crescent Down Works, whose founder worked in Eddie Bauer's down testing lab in 1969, is presented as the closest thing to old Eddie Bauer today."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The story of Eddie Bauer",
        "summary": "A preview of the video and an introduction to Eddie Bauer as the authority on down. Covers the jackets shown and the Bauers' perfectionism and personal life.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Tackle shop origins",
        "summary": "Bauer's family background in Seattle and his tackle shop restringing rackets. His shuttlecock factory got him into the feather supply chain.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Invention of the down jacket",
        "summary": "A near-fatal hypothermia episode led Bauer to design a light down jacket. It drew on Russian goose-down coats, his shuttlecock feathers and a baffled sleeping bag ad. Early adopters were bush pilots.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Wartime production",
        "summary": "WWII demand. Bauer gathered gear from other American outdoor brands, sourced feathers via Ducks Unlimited, and became a key military supplier of sleeping bags and pack boards.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "The B9 parka",
        "summary": "A detailed look at a 1943 B-9 flight parka: its down and feather fill, reinforced sleeves, double gussets, short zipper, convertible mouton collar and hood, and adjustable neck wind blocker.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "The K2 expedition",
        "summary": "The story of the Karakoram (K2) jacket designed for Seattle climbers, the failed K2 climb, and the famous belay fall arrest. Also shows a 1970s Eddie Bauer down balaclava.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 15
      },
      {
        "title": "Conquering Everest",
        "summary": "The Everest Parka made for Jim Whittaker, with nylon, velcro, heavy down fill and a wolverine and coyote fur hood, plus an anecdote from Whittaker's 50th anniversary.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 17
      },
      {
        "title": "The flying avocado era",
        "summary": "A 1970s avocado Karakoram Expedition jacket with a fur-free down hood. Covers the brand's sale in 1968 and rising competition, a wish for period-accurate reissues, and Crescent Down Works as the modern successor.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 19
      }
    ],
